DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppbbmmtteexxtt takes the specified text, either a single line from the command
       line  or  multiple lines from standard input, and renders it into a PBM
       graphical image.

       The text rendered is all the non-option command line  arguments,  sepa‐
       rated  by  spaces,  except that if there are no non-option command line
       arguments, it is Standard Input.


       In the image, each line of input is a line of output.  Lines are
         delimited by newline characters.

       The program renders any character in an input line that is not  in  the
       font
         as a space.  Note that control characters usually aren't in the font,
       but
         some fonts have glyphs for them.  The newline characters that delimit
       lines
         in of Standard Input are not in any line.

       Tab  characters  are  rendered  as a number of spaces; any entry in the
       font
         for the tab code point is irrelevant.  The number of spaces  is  such
       as to
         create tab stops every 8 characters.  Note that this is not useful if
       the
         font is proportional.


       The image is just wide enough for the longest line of text,  plus  mar‐
       gins, and just high enough to contain the lines of text, plus margins.

       The  left and right margins are twice the width of the widest character
       in the font; the top and bottom margins are the height of  the  tallest
       character  in the font.  But if the text is only one line, all the mar‐
       gins are half of this.  You can use the --nnoommaarrggiinnss option to  eliminate
       the margins.

       ppbbmmtteexxtt renders left to right.  It cannot render vertically or right to
       left.

       ppbbmmtteexxttppss does the same thing as ppbbmmtteexxtt, but uses Ghostscript to  gen‐
       erate the characters, which means you can use Postscript fonts.  But it
       also means you have to have Ghostscript installed and it isn't as fast.
       Also,  ppbbmmtteexxttppss  generates  only one line of text, whereas ppbbmmtteexxtt can
       create multiple lines.

       ppbbmmtteexxtt is meant for simple text.  If you're working with  a  _d_o_c_u_m_e_n_t,
       you  would be better off using a document formatting program to "print"
       to a Postscript file, then feeding that Postscript to ppssttooppnnmm.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common  Options  ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ), ppbbmmtteexxtt recognizes the
       following command line options:




       --wwcchhaarr

              By default, ppbbmmtteexxtt takes a single-byte character stream as  in‐
              put.   When you specify --wwcchhaarr, it treats input text as a multi‐
              byte character stream encoded according to the  current  locale.
              Normally,  the  user  would  supply  a  BDF font file encoded in
              ISO-10646-1 with a --ffoonntt option.

              With --wwcchhaarr, you cannot supply the text on the command line;  it
              must be fed from standard input.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.82 (March 2018).



       --ffoonntt

       --bbuuiillttiinn
              --bbuuiillttiinn selects a font among those built into Netpbm.

              --ffoonntt  selects  a  font  that you supply yourself either as an X
              Window    System    BDF     (Bitmap     Distribution     Format)
              ⟨http://xfree86.org/current/bdf.pdf⟩  file or as a PBM file in a
              special form.

              The default is the built in font "bdf."

              "bdf" is Times-Roman 15 pixels high.   (That's  about  14  point
              type printed at 75 dpi).

              "fixed" is a built in fixed width font.

              For  information  about other fonts, and how to make one of your
              own, see Fonts ⟨#fonts⟩  below.



       --ssppaaccee _p_i_x_e_l_s
               Add _p_i_x_e_l_s pixels of space between characters.  This is in  ad‐
              dition  to  whatever  space surrounding characters is built into
              the font, which is usually enough to produce a reasonable string
              of text.

              _p_i_x_e_l_s  may  be  fractional,  in which case the number of pixels
              added varies so as to achieve the specified average.  For  exam‐
              ple  --ssppaaccee==11..55 causes half the spaces to be 1 pixel and half to
              be 2 pixels.

              _p_i_x_e_l_s may be negative to crowd text together,  but  the  author
              has not put much thought or testing into how this works in every
              possible case, so it might cause disastrous results.


       --llssppaaccee _p_i_x_e_l_s
               Add _p_i_x_e_l_s pixels of space between lines.  This is in  addition
              to  whatever  space above and below characters is built into the
              font, which is usually enough to produce a reasonable line spac‐
              ing.

              _p_i_x_e_l_s must be a whole number.

              _p_i_x_e_l_s  may  be negative to crowd lines together, but the author
              has not put much thought or testing into how this works in every
              possible case, so it might cause disastrous results.


       --nnoommaarrggiinnss
              By  default,  ppbbmmtteexxtt  adds  margins all around the image as de‐
              scribed above.  This option causes ppbbmmtteexxtt not to add  any  mar‐
              gins.

              Note  that there may still be space beyond the edges of the type
              because a character itself may include space at its  edges.   To
              eliminate  all  surrounding  background, so the type touches all
              four edges of the image, use ppnnmmccrroopp.


       --wwiiddtthh _p_i_x_e_l_s
              This specifies how much horizontal space the text is supposed to
              fit into.

              If  the input is one line, ppbbmmtteexxtt breaks it into multiple lines
              as needed to fit the specified  width.   It  breaks  it  between
              characters,  but  does  not pay attention to white space; it may
              break in the middle of a word and a line may begin or  end  with
              white space.

              If the input is multiple lines, ppbbmmtteexxtt assumes you already have
              line breaks where they make sense, and ppbbmmtteexxtt simply  truncates
              each line as needed to fit the specified width.


       --llooaadd--eennttiirree--ffoonntt

              When  you  use  a  BDF font, ppbbmmtteexxtt will normally load from the
              font
                  only the characters needed for your text, not  wasting  time
              loading other
                  characters.  With this option, ppbbmmtteexxtt will instead read the
              entire
                  font.  It won't make any difference in the rendered  output,
              but it lets
                  you check the integrity of the font file.

              This  option  was new in Netpbm 10.91 (June 2020).  Before that,
              ppbbmmtteexxtt always reads the entire font.


       --vveerrbboossee
              This makes ppbbmmtteexxtt issue informtional messages  about  its  pro‐
              cessing.


       --ddrryy--rruunn

              With  this  option,  instead of outputting an image of the text,
              ppbbmmtteexxtt just writes to Standard Output a message telling the di‐
              mensions of the image it would have produced.

              You can specify only one of --ddrryy--rruunn and --tteexxtt--dduummpp.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.75 (June 2016).


       --tteexxtt--dduummpp
              This  option causes ppbbmmtteexxtt just to write to Standard Output the
              text in ASCII that would be rendered.  The output  reflects  any
              text  formatting, unprintable character substitution, tab expan‐
              sion, etc.  It is for diagnosing problems.  This option was  new
              in Netpbm 10.82 (March 2018).

              When  --wwcchhaarr is in effect, the output text will be in the encod‐
              ing specified by the current locale.

              You can specify only one of --ddrryy--rruunn and --tteexxtt--dduummpp.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.82 (March 2018).





UUSSAAGGEE
       Often, you want to place text over another image.  One way to  do  this
       is  with  ppppmmllaabbeell.  For more flexible (but complex) drawing of text on
       an image, there is ppppmmddrraaww.  These do not give  you  the  font  options
       that ppbbmmtteexxtt does, though.

       Another  way  is to use ppbbmmtteexxtt to create an image containing the text,
       then use ppaammccoommpp to overlay the text image onto your  base  image.   To
       make  only  the text (and not the entire rectangle containing it) cover
       the base image, you will need to give ppaammccoommpp a mask,  via  its  --aallpphhaa
       option.  You can just use the text image itself as the mask, as long as
       you also specify the --iinnvveerrtt option to ppaammccoommpp.

       If you want to overlay colored text instead of  black,  just  use  ppppmm‐‐
       cchhaannggee  to  change  all black pixels to the color of your choice before
       overlaying the text image.  But still use the original black and  white
       image for the transparency mask.

       If  you want the text at an angle, use ppnnmmrroottaattee on the text image (and
       transparency mask) before overlaying.


